---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Reading — Stevie Thomas"
  description="The books and lines of research that shaped how I think about skill, variability, and measurement."
>
  <section style="padding: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 0 0;">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal" style="--i:0">Reading</p>
    <h1 class="reveal" style="--i:1">What shaped the way I think about this</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Not a bibliography, and not complete. These are the things that actually changed how I
        coach, in roughly the order they got to me. I have tried to describe each one honestly
        rather than sell it.
      </p>

      <h3>Ecological dynamics and the constraints-led approach</h3>
      <p>
        The line of work running from Bernstein through Gibson to the modern ecological dynamics
        literature — and, for baseball specifically, Rob Gray's writing and podcast — is the single
        biggest influence on how I run a practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core move is to stop treating skill as a stored program the athlete executes and start
        treating it as an ongoing fit between a performer and an environment. Change the
        environment and you change the movement, without ever describing the movement. That is a
        completely different job than "teach the correct positions", and it explains a lot of
        coaching that works but that nobody can justify in positional language.
      </p>
      <p>
        Newell's framing of constraints — organism, task, environment — is the most useful practical
        handle on it. Most of what a coach actually controls is the task constraint.
      </p>

      <h3>Perception and action as one loop</h3>
      <p>
        Michaels and Carello's <em>Direct Perception</em>, and the perception-action work that came
        out of that tradition — Warren on how visual information governs action, Fajen on
        affordance-based control, Turvey on coordination.
      </p>
      <p>
        For hitting the implication is severe: a swing rehearsed without a real pitch to perceive is
        a different task, not an easier version of the same one. Tee work is not swing practice with
        the pitch removed. It is a separate skill that overlaps somewhat.
      </p>

      <h3>Variability as structure, not noise</h3>
      <p>
        The motor control literature on movement variability — the uncontrolled manifold work and
        everything around it. The idea that a skilled performer is not the most repeatable one, but
        the one whose variability is organised so that the parts that do not matter are free to
        move.
      </p>
      <p>
        This reframed the readouts for me entirely. A hitter with high swing-to-swing spread is not
        automatically inconsistent. They might be adjusting, which is the thing you wanted. Telling
        those two apart is most of what I work on.
      </p>

      <h3>Attention and how instruction lands</h3>
      <p>
        Wulf and Lewthwaite's OPTIMAL theory, and the broader external-focus literature. The finding
        that directing attention outward — to the effect of the movement rather than the body part
        making it — generally produces better performance and better learning is one of the most
        replicated results in the field, and one of the most ignored in practice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost every cue a coach reaches for by instinct is internal. Rewriting them as external is
        the cheapest improvement available to most coaching, and it costs nothing but the habit.
      </p>

      <h3>Neuroplasticity, with a caveat</h3>
      <p>
        Doidge's <em>The Brain That Changes Itself</em> is the accessible entry point and worth
        reading. My caveat is that popular neuroplasticity writing invites a leap — from "the brain
        changes" to "therefore this specific drill rewires it" — that the underlying work does not
        support. Useful as orientation, not as a warrant.
      </p>

      <h3>Run value, and thinking in expectation</h3>
      <p>
        <em>The Book</em> by Tango, Lichtman and Dolphin. The specific tactical conclusions matter
        less to me than the habit it builds: evaluate a decision by its expected run value in the
        situation, not by whether it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        That habit is why I ended up looking at how much the value of an outcome depends on the base
        state around it — which turned out to be far more than the way we talk about hitters
        suggests.
      </p>

      <h3>Where I think the gap is</h3>
      <p>
        The ecological and motor-learning literature is strong on principle and thin on prescription
        for a specific athlete on a specific Tuesday. The biomechanics literature is strong on
        measurement and thin on what to do about it. Most of my projects live in that gap, and most
        of them fail there, which is roughly what you would expect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
